<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is... – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present a summary of information depicted in a book or article. Interactive: Participate in group discussions on current events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present a summary of academic advice from an article, highlighting key points and examples (e.g., "The article explains that if you organize your notes, you will study more effectively. It also suggests taking regular breaks.").
Can participate in group discussions about study habits, sharing personal tips and advice (e.g., "I think using flashcards really helps. Rubiela recommended that I should try them for vocabulary practice.").
Can express agreement or disagreement during discussions on academic success strategies, providing reasons for their opinions (e.g., "I agree that setting goals is important because it helps me stay focused." "I don't think studying late at night is effective, since I get too tired.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Think-Pair-Share" activity. The teacher asks, "What was one academic challenge you faced in 9th grade, and what advice do you wish someone had given you?" Students briefly think individually, then share their ideas and listen to their partner's responses for 2 minutes each. This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to 'academic achievement' and 'advice'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models giving advice using target grammar: modals and the second conditional. For example, "If I were a ninth grader again, I would definitely organize my study time better." and "You should always set clear academic goals." The teacher highlights the pronunciation of key vocabulary like 'growth' /grəʊθ/ versus 'create' /kriˈeɪt/, and 'progress' (noun: PRO-gress) versus 'progress' (verb: pro-GRESS) while saying, "To show PRO-gress, you need to pro-GRESS consistently."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target grammar and concept of advice.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "If I say, 'You should review your notes daily,' am I telling you what you *must* do, or what is a good idea?" (Expected answer: "A good idea.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "If I say, 'If I studied more, I would get better grades,' am I talking about a real situation now, or a hypothetical situation?" (Expected answer: "A hypothetical situation.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Review of key academic advice points from a short article summary previously read in Lesson 2 (e.g., "The Importance of Effective Study Habits for Ninth Graders"). The teacher displays a bulleted list of 5-6 main pieces of advice (e.g., "Organize your time effectively," "Set clear goals," "Seek feedback," "Practice consistently").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will understand and recall key information presented verbally and visually about academic advice and discuss it to prepare for speaking tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What was the main purpose of the article we read in the last class?" and "What were two key strategies the article recommended for academic improvement?" Students share their answers verbally with a partner, recalling information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher projects a few statements. Students match each statement with the advice category from the article (e.g., "Using a planner helps you track assignments" -> "Time Management"). Students call out answers.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice giving academic advice using the second conditional ("If I were more organized, I would achieve my academic goals."), modals for advice ("You should complete your assignments on time."), and the first conditional ("If you focus on your studies, you will achieve your academic goals."). They will also use target vocabulary: 'improvement', 'strategy', 'focus', 'discipline', 'time management'. Focus on the contrastive sounds /gr/ and /kr/, and stress patterns for 'progress'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice – Advice Chain:</b> The teacher starts by posing a common 9th-grade problem (e.g., "I find it hard to understand difficult concepts."). The first student uses a modal or conditional to give advice (e.g., "You should ask questions in class."). The next student then gives alternative advice using a different structure or vocabulary (e.g., "If you brainstorm ideas with a friend, you might understand better."). The chain continues, encouraging the use of varied vocabulary and grammar. The teacher listens for accurate pronunciation of words like 'growth' and 'create' when students use them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice – Role-play Prompts:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides scenario cards with typical 9th-grade academic challenges (e.g., "Procrastinating on projects," "Struggling with English vocabulary," "Feeling unmotivated"). Students take turns role-playing: one describes a challenge, and the other offers advice using the target grammar and vocabulary (e.g., "What study strategies do you recommend to improve my grades?"). They should use phrases like "You must focus on your goals," "If I were you, I would try using flashcards," or "If you organize your notes, you will make good PRO-gress." The teacher circulates, offering immediate feedback on grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation, explicitly correcting stress on "progress" if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction – Peer Advice Exchange:</b> Students switch partners. Each student shares a personal current academic challenge (e.g., "I want to improve my speaking skills but feel shy."). Their partner actively listens and provides two pieces of actionable advice using the second conditional, modals, or first conditional. They also practice asking for clarification politely (e.g., "Could you explain what you mean by 'active learning style'?"). This fosters sociolinguistic competence by using inclusive and encouraging language.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Presentation. Students work in groups of four. They will collaboratively discuss and compile "My Top 3 Pieces of Advice for a Ninth Grader."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Each student in the group shares their individual best advice, explaining why they think it's important (e.g., "I think using a planner is essential because it helps with time management. If you don't track your deadlines, you will procrastinate."). They must use at least one modal for advice or a conditional sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Groups engage in a discussion to agree on their "Top 3 Pieces of Advice." They must use language for agreement/disagreement and facilitating discussion (e.g., "I agree that setting goals is important," "I don't think studying late at night is effective, since I get too tired," "Let's share study techniques that have worked for us.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Each group chooses a spokesperson to present their Top 3 advice points to the class, summarizing their group's reasoning and including examples (e.g., "Our group recommends that a ninth grader should organize their notes because if they do, they will study more effectively. We also suggest that if you practice English daily, your speaking skills will improve."). The spokesperson focuses on presenting clearly and intelligibly.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation using a checklist during group discussions and presentations. Peer assessment during group work. The teacher collects "exit tickets" for self-assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in group discussions and their designated spokesperson presents the group's compiled academic advice to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of target sounds and stress patterns).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'strategy', 'motivation', 'focus', 'achievement').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of second conditional, modals for advice, and first conditional.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence when presenting advice and reasons.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative participation in group discussions (e.g., asking for advice, giving feedback, expressing agreement/disagreement).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket answering the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One new piece of academic advice I learned today is...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. I felt confident using (modal/conditional phrase) when giving advice today. (e.g. "I felt confident using 'You should...'")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was challenging about giving advice to your peers, and how did you overcome it?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. How will the advice discussed today help you achieve your future academic goals?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities effectively encouraged the use of modals and conditionals for advice. Students showed good engagement in pair and group discussions. Some students still need more practice with the pronunciation of specific sounds like /gr/ and the noun/verb stress for 'progress'. I will ensure to integrate quick pronunciation drills in the next lesson's warm-up and provide targeted feedback. Adjustments for next lesson: Provide more explicit prompts for using complex sentences to express nuanced advice. For Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), I will guide students to structure their written advice clearly, building on the oral summaries created today, focusing on cohesion and specific vocabulary from the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The summaries of academic advice developed and presented today will serve as a foundation for students to write a short advice column or letter to a ninth grader in the next lesson, focusing on structuring their ideas in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>